# Adjudicated `punused` findings for deadset-go. One reason per group; entries
# match as literal substrings of the finding line, so they never include
# file:line:col -- line numbers shift and an entry with one silently goes stale.
#
# `punused` resolves references through gopls inside this module only and does
# not model interface satisfaction, so two whole classes below are its known
# blind spots rather than dead code. Cross-checked at adjudication time:
# `deadcode -test ./...` reports nothing in this module.

# --- Fixture modules under internal/load/testdata/.
#
# Each of these is a declaration inside a fixture module the loader's own tests
# load and analyze. They are deliberately unreferenced: the fixtures exist to be
# read by a package loader, not called, and two of them are deliberately
# ill-formed (a type mismatch and an undefined identifier) so the fail-closed
# error walk has something to walk. Go's own tooling ignores a `testdata`
# directory; this scan does not, so the fixtures surface here as findings.
# Deleting any of them would break the test that names it.
function Selected
function Answer
function Mismatch
function Undefined

# --- Methods a standard interface calls, never a caller by name.
#
# `Error()` on both error types satisfies `error`, and production code returns
# them as `error`; `String()` on the kind enumerations satisfies `fmt.Stringer`
# and is what renders a kind in a message and in a report. No call site exists
# for a reference search to find in either case, because the runtime dispatches
# through the interface. Removing one would not fail the build, it would silently
# change what a diagnostic prints, which is why each is pinned by a test of its
# rendered output.
method (*Error).Error
method (ErrorKind).String
method (SymbolKind).String
method (RefKind).String

# --- The stages no verb calls yet, exercised by tests until one does.
#
# `print-roots` wires the loader, the scope of a directory, the enumeration and
# the root detection into a production path, and `print-retained` wires the
# reference pass, the resolver, the exemption classes and the sweep, so those
# stages have callers. One does not: no verb takes a scope document, so nothing
# calls the reader that decodes one. It is named in the plan for the verb that
# lands it, and the correct resolution is that call site rather than an unexport:
# the packages are separate so that one stage's failure cannot break another's
# compile, and a stage's entry point is its package's surface.
function Read

# --- The per-analyzer configuration section.
#
# `Go` is the section of the configuration document this analyzer owns, declared
# in the published schema and therefore accepted by the decode. Nothing reads it
# yet because no setting inside it has an implementation; the field must exist
# for a document that carries the section to resolve rather than be refused.
field Go is unused

# --- What the sweep answers and no verb reports yet.
#
# `print-retained` indexes the graph from the enumeration, the reference set and
# the root set and sweeps it, so the index and the sweep have callers. What no
# verb reads yet is a dead component: the listing at the depth the run asks for,
# and the two spellings a report and a message carry for a relation and a cascade.
# Only the tests call them today, and the correct resolution is the reporting verb
# rather than an unexport, because each is a stage of the same package the
# enumeration and the roots are stages of.
method (*Component).List
method (Relation).String
method (Cascade).String
